        "In 1931 historian James Truslow Adams coined the phrase **the American Dream**: the meritocratic promise that hard work earns independence, dignity and land ownership regardless of birth. Steinbeck uses George Milton and Lennie Small to test — and shatter — that Jeffersonian agrarian myth under 1930s capitalism.\n\n### Character Contrasts & Symbiosis\n- **Physical antithesis**: George is `'small and quick, dark of face, with restless eyes and sharp, strong features'`, whereas Lennie is `'a huge man, shapeless of face'` who walks `'dragging his feet a little, the way a bear drags his paws'`.\n- **Zoomorphism**: Steinbeck saturates Lennie's portrayal with animal imagery — a `'bear'`, a `'horse'` snorting in the pool, a `'terrier'` fetching the dead mouse, `'strong as a bull'`, bleating like a `'lamb'`. Far from degrading Lennie, this **zoomorphism** signals pre-moral innocence alongside uncontrollable physical force in a predatory world that destroys the vulnerable.\n- **1930s attitudes to intellectual disability (AO4)**: In an era before community care — when men like Lennie risked institutionalisation in `'a cage'` at an asylum or vigilante lynching (`'strung up'` in Weed and by Curley's mob) — George acts as Lennie's sole buffer against a brutal society.\n\n### The Farm Dream as Secular Liturgy\nNotice how George and Lennie perform the dream like a church call-and-response (`'Tell how it's gonna be'`). Their ten-acre Eden — a little house, alfalfa patch, stove in winter, `'live off the fatta the lan''`, where `'nobody gonna hurt nobody nor steal from 'em'` — promises **autonomy** (`'we wouldn't have to buck no barley for nobody'`), **security** and **belonging**.\n\n| Stage in Novella | Status of the Dream | Steinbeck's Structural Point |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Section 1 (Salinas Pool)** | Spoken ritual; pure comforting myth. | Distinguishes them (`'With us it ain't like that'`) from lonely ranch hands. |\n| **Section 3 (Bunkhouse)** | **Candy** pledges **$350** toward the **$600** farm. | Peak hope: even sceptical George marvels, `'I bet we could swing her'` — making the subsequent fall genuinely tragic. |\n| **Section 4 (Crooks's Room)** | Crooks warns `'Nobody never gets to heaven, and nobody gets no land'`. | Contextual voice of realism: hundreds of bindle-stiffs carry `'that same damn thing in their heads'`. |\n| **Section 5–6 (Barn & Pool)** | George admits `'I think I knowed from the very first'` it would fail; recites it one final time before firing Carlson's Luger. | The American Dream survives only as a merciful bedtime illusion before death. |",
