        "Section 4 is Steinbeck's sharpest **sociological X-ray** of 1930s America. Unlike transient barley buckers, both **Crooks** and **Candy** are permanent ranch fixtures precisely because disability and race bar them from competing on the open labour market.\n\n### Crooks: Segregation, Literacy & Jim Crow Terror (AO4)\n- **Spatial segregation**: Excluded from the white bunkhouse because of racial prejudice (`'They say I stink'`), the `'proud, aloof'` stable buck lives in a harness shed off the barn alongside leather, manure and liniment for his crooked spine.\n- **Symbolic props**: Crooks owns `'several pairs of shoes'`, a `'tattered dictionary'` and **`'a mauled copy of the California civil code for 1905'`**.\n  - *AO1 + AO4 gold*: Even though California was not a Deep South state, *de facto* **Jim Crow segregation** and extra-legal lynching terrorised Black Americans nationwide in the 1930s. That `'mauled'` 1905 law book proves Crooks knows his theoretical constitutional rights by heart — yet Curley's wife's single threat (`'strung up on a tree so easy it ain't even funny'`) instantly overrides the law, forcing Crooks to `'reduce himself to nothing'`.\n- **Weaponised loneliness**: Before warming to Lennie, Crooks cruelly torments him with the thought that George might never return from town — forcing Lennie to taste the chronic isolation Crooks endures every night (`'A guy goes nuts if he ain't got nobody... a guy gets too lonely an' he gets sick'`).\n\n### Candy & His Sheepdog: Capitalist Disposability (AO4)\n- **Allegory of utility**: Having lost his right hand on this very ranch, `'stoop-shouldered'` Candy sweeps floors as a `'swamper'` and dreads the day they `'can me'` (`'put me on the county'`, before comprehensive New Deal Social Security protected farm labourers, who were excluded from the 1935 Social Security Act!).\n- **Carlson's shooting of Candy's dog (Section 3)**: Carlson insists the blind, foul-smelling dog `'ain't no good to himself'` — cold utilitarian logic where aging workers and animals are discarded once unproductive. Candy's haunted regret (`'I ought to of shot that dog myself, George. I shouldn't ought to of let no stranger shoot my dog'`) plants the moral seed for George's mercy killing of Lennie in Section 6.",
