      body:
        "Even though Edexcel 4ET1 Paper 1 Section B does not mark AO2 separately, analysing Steinbeck's **macro-structure** earns top-band **AO1** marks for critical insight and connects directly to **AO4 naturalistic determinism**.\n\n### The Play-Novelette Architecture\nEach of the six sections opens with theatrical **stage-direction prose** establishing lighting, sound and spatial boundaries before characters enter:\n- **Section 1 (Thursday evening — Salinas brush)**: Edenic golden light on the Gabilan foothills; a water snake glides safely across the green pool; George tells Lennie `'Hide in the brush till I come for you'`.\n- **Sections 2–5 (Friday morning to Sunday afternoon — Ranch interiors)**: Progressive spatial constriction — from the communal **bunkhouse** (Secs 2–3), to **Crooks's cramped lean-to** (Sec 4), to the hushed **barn stall** (Sec 5) while horseshoes clang carelessly outside.\n- **Section 6 (Sunday dusk — Salinas brush)**: Identical pool, inverted mood. Shadows lengthen; a motionless **heron** lances down and swallows the little water snake `'while its tail waved frantically'`; Lennie hallucinates **Aunt Clara** and a **gigantic rabbit** voicing his guilt before George arrives.\n\n### Escalating Foreshadowing Chain (Fatalism)\nNothing in Section 6 is an accident. Steinbeck constructs a relentless **chain of inevitability**:\n1. **Weed flashback** (grabbed red dress -> panicked -> lynch mob) foreshadows **Section 5** (stroked Curley's wife's hair -> panicked -> Curley's lynch mob).\n2. **Escalating fragile victims**: dead mice (Sec 1) -> crushed Curley's hand `'flopping like a fish'` (Sec 3) -> dead puppy (Sec 5) -> Curley's wife's body `'flopped like a fish'` (Sec 5).\n3. **Candy's dog & Carlson's Luger**: Carlson shoots the old dog in the back of the head so it feels no pain (Sec 3); Candy wishes he had done it himself rather than leaving it to a stranger; George uses **Carlson's stolen Luger** at the exact same spot on Lennie's skull to spare him Curley's shotgun (`'shoot him in the guts'`) or an asylum cage.\n\n### The Chilling Final Line\nAfter Slim gently leads devastated George away (`'A guy got to sometimes'`), **Carlson** asks Curley: **`'Now what the hell ya suppose is eatin' them two guys?'`** Ending on Carlson's emotional blindness delivers Steinbeck's ultimate contextual indictment: 1930s ranch survival has so thoroughly numbed men to human attachment that grief over a friend is literally incomprehensible.",
